# Normalise to LF in the working tree.
#
# The hosted Windows runners check out with core.autocrlf on, which rewrites
# every text file to CRLF. gofmt normalises to LF, so a CRLF checkout makes
# `gofmt -l` report every file it touches and the formatting check fails on code
# that is correctly formatted. That failure says nothing about the code, which
# is the worst kind: it trains people to ignore the check.
#
# Pinning the line endings here means the same bytes arrive on every runner, so
# the check reports formatting and only formatting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# These are read by PowerShell on Windows. LF is fine there, and keeping one rule
# for everything is simpler than a rule that has to be remembered.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf

# Binaries staged by the build, never committed.
*.exe binary
*.zip binary
*.dat binary
*.dll binary
